From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Let a Unix-launched process join session 0, and never start a shell there

A shared system prefix has a machine-level wineserver running before anyone
logs in. On Windows the Windows system runs in session 0, on a
non-interactive window station, and gets no shell. Wine already implements
exactly that rule -- get_desktop_window() checks for
__wineservice_winstation and has the server create the desktop window rather
than starting explorer.

It could not be reached from here. A process's station comes from
STARTUPINFO.lpDesktop, set by its parent, and our Windows system is started
by a script rather than by a parent Windows process. services.exe sets it for
the services it launches but never for itself, because on Windows its parent
does that. So every session-0 process landed on the interactive WinSta0 and
Wine started an explorer for it. The logged-in user's explorer then shared a
wineserver with that one: two shells in one prefix, and the taskbar laid out
for whichever screen size got there first.

winstation_init() gains a fallback to SG_WINSTATION, which supplies the same
string lpDesktop would. It is read only when the process parameter is absent,
so nothing started by a Windows parent changes behaviour. The environment
block is walked by hand because this file builds into win32u.so, the Unix
side, which does not import ntdll's PE exports.

The second hunk is a safety property, not a feature. Upstream reaches the
explorer path only when the server declined to create the desktop window,
which for a service means the station or desktop name is wrong -- and
starting explorer then is actively harmful, because that explorer inherits
the same environment, hits the same failure and starts another. A malformed
SG_WINSTATION forks until the machine dies; this was not hypothetical, a
doubled backslash in a shell literal did it. A service has no shell to lose,
so declining is both correct and safe.

Forwarded: not-needed
--- a/dlls/win32u/winstation.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/winstation.c
@@ -800,6 +800,20 @@
     }
     SERVER_END_REQ;
 
+    /* A service never starts a shell. Upstream reaches the explorer path below
+     * only when the server declined to create the desktop window, which for a
+     * service means something is wrong with the station or desktop name -- and
+     * starting explorer then is actively harmful, because the explorer inherits
+     * the same environment, reaches the same failure and starts another. A bad
+     * SG_WINSTATION value forks until the machine dies. Refuse instead: session
+     * 0 has no interactive shell on Windows either, so there is nothing to lose
+     * by declining, and a misconfigured fleet machine stays usable. */
+    if (!thread_info->top_window && is_service)
+    {
+        WARN( "no desktop window for a session 0 process; not starting a shell\n" );
+        return 0;
+    }
+
     if (!thread_info->top_window)
     {
         static const WCHAR appnameW[] = {'\\','?','?','\\','C',':','\\','w','i','n','d','o','w','s',
@@ -974,6 +988,46 @@
         buffer = malloc( params->Desktop.Length + sizeof(WCHAR) );
         memcpy( buffer, params->Desktop.Buffer, params->Desktop.Length );
         buffer[params->Desktop.Length / sizeof(WCHAR)] = 0;
+    }
+    else
+    {
+        /* A process started from a Unix shell has no Desktop process parameter,
+         * so it always lands on the interactive WinSta0. Windows has no such
+         * gap -- a service is put on a non-interactive station by whoever starts
+         * it -- but there is no way to say so from outside Wine, and the Windows
+         * system of a shared prefix is started by a script, not by a parent
+         * Windows process.
+         *
+         * SG_WINSTATION supplies the same string STARTUPINFO.lpDesktop would.
+         * Setting it to __wineservice_winstation\\Default puts the process in
+         * session 0, which get_desktop_window() already handles: it creates the
+         * desktop window itself rather than starting an explorer. Without that,
+         * a machine-level wineserver grows a shell of its own, and a logged-in
+         * user's explorer then shares a prefix with it -- two shells, one
+         * wineserver, and a taskbar laid out for whichever screen size won. */
+        static const WCHAR sg_winstationW[] =
+            {'S','G','_','W','I','N','S','T','A','T','I','O','N','=',0};
+        const WCHAR *env = params->Environment;
+        const size_t len = ARRAY_SIZE(sg_winstationW) - 1;
+
+        /* Walked by hand rather than with RtlQueryEnvironmentVariable_U: this
+         * file is built into win32u.so, the Unix side, which does not import
+         * ntdll's PE exports. */
+        while (env && *env)
+        {
+            size_t n = wcslen( env );
+            if (n > len && !wcsncmp( env, sg_winstationW, len ))
+            {
+                buffer = malloc( (n - len + 1) * sizeof(WCHAR) );
+                memcpy( buffer, env + len, (n - len + 1) * sizeof(WCHAR) );
+                break;
+            }
+            env += n + 1;
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (buffer)
+    {
         if ((desktop = wcschr( buffer, '\\' )))
         {
             *desktop++ = 0;
